KNOWNBUG
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ pointer to integer: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ pointer to unrelated pointer: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ cast away const: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [expr.type.conv]/1.1: with a single expression, T(e) is equivalent to
the cast expression (T)e, which [expr.cast]/4 may interpret as a
reinterpret_cast or a const_cast.  When T is a typedef-name, the front end
resolves T(e) as constructor overload resolution and rejects these
conversions ("found no match for symbol 'U', candidates are: constructor U
(), constructor U (U)").  `(U)e' and `long(e)' work.  libstdc++'s
__waiter_pool_base::_S_for computes `uintptr_t(__addr)', so its body is
dropped (bits/atomic_wait.h:252 with GCC 13) for <atomic>, <thread>,
<stop_token>, <future>, <condition_variable> and <memory>.  Pre-existing
(bin53).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program (-std=c++98), and it
fails only its WRONG assertion.
